{
{% for post in site.posts %}
{% comment %}
  Two decode passes, and the order matters.

  Markdown re-escapes a literal entity in the source, so a post whose text
  mentions `&quot;` renders as `&amp;quot;`. A single pass that ends with
  `&amp;` -> `&` therefore decodes only one layer and leaves `&quot;` in the
  corpus: visible on 2020-07-06 Visual Studio-类视图和对象浏览器图标 and
  2020-06-30 开篇·序, where the 2026-09-25 proofread note quotes the entity by
  name. Decoding `&amp;` first and running the list twice unwraps that layer.

  The second pass leaves `&#183;` alone -- it is not a name on the list -- so a
  note that *discusses* an entity still reads correctly instead of being
  silently rewritten.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign search_text = post.content | strip_html | normalize_whitespace %}
{% assign search_text = search_text | replace: '&amp;', '&' %}
{% assign search_text = search_text | replace: '&lt;', '<' %}
{% assign search_text = search_text | replace: '&gt;', '>' %}
{% assign search_text = search_text | replace: '&quot;', '"' %}
{% assign search_text = search_text | replace: '&#39;', "'" %}
{% assign search_text = search_text | replace: '&#x27;', "'" %}
{% assign search_text = search_text | replace: '&amp;', '&' %}
{% assign search_text = search_text | replace: '&lt;', '<' %}
{% assign search_text = search_text | replace: '&gt;', '>' %}
{% assign search_text = search_text | replace: '&quot;', '"' %}
{{ post.url | jsonify }}: {{ search_text | jsonify }}{% unless forloop.last %},{% endunless %}
{% endfor %}
}